1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:02,076
You wanted to be a pirate.

2
00:00:02,076 --> 00:00:05,189
Gold, freedom, a magnificent coat.

3
00:00:05,189 --> 00:00:09,589
Instead, you are holding a bucket below
deck while somebody above

4
00:00:09,589 --> 00:00:13,292
you asks whether you have finished
cleaning the ship's most disgusting

5
00:00:13,292 --> 00:00:17,274
corner. The magnificent coat belongs to
someone else.

6
00:00:17,274 --> 00:00:21,131
Your shirt has a patch, your breakfast
could damage a tooth, and

7
00:00:21,131 --> 00:00:24,722
the bucket is yours. Congratulations: you
have

8
00:00:24,722 --> 00:00:28,749
successfully escaped ordinary employment
into unusually

9
00:00:28,749 --> 00:00:32,937
dangerous employment. Imagine you are Tom,
an adult

10
00:00:33,437 --> 00:00:36,015
sailor in the Caribbean around seventeen
fifteen.

11
00:00:36,030 --> 00:00:40,141
Your crew and personal journey are
fictional, but the problems come

12
00:00:40,141 --> 00:00:43,807
from the history of seafaring during
piracy's so-called golden age.

13
00:00:44,707 --> 00:00:47,500
That age was golden mainly for later
storytellers.

14
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:53,059
Royal Museums Greenwich places the
familiar Atlantic

15
00:00:53,059 --> 00:00:55,350
boom between the sixteen eighties and
seventeen twenties.

16
00:00:56,284 --> 00:00:59,339
It was not a comfortable retirement
program with optional sword

17
00:00:59,339 --> 00:01:03,519
fights. You begin ashore, carrying your
belongings in a small

18
00:01:03,519 --> 00:01:07,738
bundle. You know ships, hard work, and
disappointing wages.

19
00:01:08,316 --> 00:01:10,239
Work at sea could be harsh.

20
00:01:10,239 --> 00:01:14,505
The museum describes poor pay and heavy
labor in merchant

21
00:01:14,505 --> 00:01:17,369
shipping, alongside severe naval
discipline.

22
00:01:17,369 --> 00:01:20,300
Piracy promised some sailors another way
out.

23
00:01:21,087 --> 00:01:23,139
A promise is not a guarantee.

24
00:01:23,139 --> 00:01:26,871
Nor did every person aboard a pirate
vessel join

25
00:01:26,871 --> 00:01:31,073
enthusiastically. Captured crews might
supply recruits, and coercion

26
00:01:31,073 --> 00:01:34,988
complicated the picture. Your imagined
choice is

27
00:01:34,988 --> 00:01:39,247
voluntary. That does not make everybody
else's experience the

28
00:01:39,247 --> 00:01:42,787
same. And these were criminals taking
other people's property,

29
00:01:42,787 --> 00:01:44,674
sometimes their freedom or lives.

30
00:01:45,429 --> 00:01:49,009
Understanding the appeal does not turn the
victims into scenery.

31
00:01:49,523 --> 00:01:53,265
The adventure you are considering has
someone else's fear inside it.

32
00:01:54,004 --> 00:01:57,525
At a waterfront tavern, an experienced
sailor describes a crew

33
00:01:57,525 --> 00:02:01,909
preparing to sail. There could be a share
of captured wealth, he

34
00:02:01,909 --> 00:02:06,213
says. You notice he describes the wealth
more carefully than the

35
00:02:06,213 --> 00:02:08,444
work. You ask about the vessel.

36
00:02:08,444 --> 00:02:11,776
It is a modest wooden sloop, not a
floating palace with five

37
00:02:11,783 --> 00:02:14,956
decks of cannons. Pirates used different
ships.

38
00:02:14,956 --> 00:02:19,314
Your fictional crew's boat is small enough
to feel everybody's elbows.

39
00:02:19,314 --> 00:02:22,554
Down at the quay, the quartermaster meets
you.

40
00:02:22,554 --> 00:02:26,599
In some pirate crews, this officer helped
manage supplies and

41
00:02:26,599 --> 00:02:30,830
represent crew interests. The title did
not mean you were about to

42
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:34,262
receive hotel service. He asks what you
can do.

43
00:02:34,262 --> 00:02:37,424
You know the basic work of a sailor and
can help keep

44
00:02:37,424 --> 00:02:41,397
a vessel running. The captain stands
nearby, looking considerably more

45
00:02:41,397 --> 00:02:45,365
important than you. Nobody hands you a
feathered hat and instantly

46
00:02:45,365 --> 00:02:47,077
promotes your opinions into policy.

47
00:02:47,814 --> 00:02:52,311
You inspect the deck, mast, sail, and
rigging.

48
00:02:52,311 --> 00:02:56,210
The ropes are not decorative lines
surrounding an adventure.

49
00:02:56,210 --> 00:03:00,186
They are equipment that somebody must
understand, handle,

50
00:03:00,186 --> 00:03:04,541
maintain, and replace before trouble makes
the lesson

51
00:03:04,541 --> 00:03:07,280
memorable. Below deck, the space is
cramped.

52
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,697
There are supplies, personal belongings,
and the unmistakable smell of

53
00:03:11,697 --> 00:03:15,909
a working ship. You place your bundle
beside the sleeping area.

54
00:03:15,909 --> 00:03:19,531
Another sailor moves it slightly because
it is in his way.

55
00:03:19,531 --> 00:03:23,927
Your first territorial conflict involves
approximately enough space to

56
00:03:23,927 --> 00:03:25,221
put down one tired human.

57
00:03:25,974 --> 00:03:27,998
Then the crew's rules are explained.

58
00:03:27,998 --> 00:03:32,203
Many pirate companies had agreements,
often called articles,

59
00:03:32,203 --> 00:03:35,070
covering conduct and the division of
proceeds.

60
00:03:35,070 --> 00:03:39,296
They were practical arrangements, not the
identical universal

61
00:03:39,296 --> 00:03:42,979
code from every movie. Some surviving
accounts include

62
00:03:42,979 --> 00:03:46,992
compensation for injury and rules about
disputes or

63
00:03:46,992 --> 00:03:51,300
gambling. The details differed. Your
imagined crew has

64
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:54,883
its own bargain; we are not going to
invent a standard pirate

65
00:03:54,883 --> 00:03:59,339
benefits package. The promise of a share
can sound better than

66
00:03:59,339 --> 00:04:03,267
a poor wage. But a share of disappointing
proceeds remains

67
00:04:03,267 --> 00:04:07,722
disappointing. If the expedition earns
little, arithmetic will not

68
00:04:07,722 --> 00:04:10,943
create a magnificent fortune out of
enthusiasm.

69
00:04:10,943 --> 00:04:15,326
Captains could be chosen by crews, and
some could be removed.

70
00:04:15,326 --> 00:04:19,319
It does not mean every vessel was equally
democratic,

71
00:04:19,319 --> 00:04:21,926
gentle, or welcoming to everybody.

72
00:04:21,926 --> 00:04:26,222
Authority still mattered, especially
during dangerous encounters.

73
00:04:26,222 --> 00:04:29,534
You might have a voice in a decision
without being free to

74
00:04:29,534 --> 00:04:31,357
wander away from your assigned work.

75
00:04:32,207 --> 00:04:35,079
Freedom apparently comes with a rope that
needs handling.

76
00:04:35,833 --> 00:04:40,019
And an agreement cannot remove prejudice,
violence, or unequal

77
00:04:40,019 --> 00:04:44,320
power. Pirates included people from
different backgrounds, but

78
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:48,778
the romantic idea of universal equality
does not survive a

79
00:04:48,778 --> 00:04:50,641
close look at the wider history.

80
00:04:51,395 --> 00:04:54,606
The quartermaster checks provisions while
you carry a water container

81
00:04:54,606 --> 00:04:58,026
aboard. Water matters before treasure
does.

82
00:04:58,026 --> 00:05:02,390
A ship can go surprisingly far on
ambition, provided somebody

83
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:05,032
has remembered the food and drink as well.

84
00:05:05,032 --> 00:05:08,121
You help stow sacks and barrels securely.

85
00:05:08,121 --> 00:05:09,894
There is only so much room.

86
00:05:09,894 --> 00:05:14,371
Extra supplies compete with equipment,
crew, and any cargo you

87
00:05:14,467 --> 00:05:18,552
hope to take later. A sailor shows you the
tools used for

88
00:05:18,552 --> 00:05:22,916
ordinary repairs. Wood, rope, cloth, and
iron

89
00:05:22,916 --> 00:05:26,998
fittings all wear. You have joined an
armed crew, but much of

90
00:05:26,998 --> 00:05:29,060
your success will depend on maintenance.

91
00:05:29,702 --> 00:05:33,980
Weapons are present too. They are
dangerous objects in

92
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:36,907
crowded hands, not a guarantee that their
owner will win.

93
00:05:37,806 --> 00:05:41,722
You would prefer every confrontation to
end before anybody discovers

94
00:05:41,818 --> 00:05:43,853
how vulnerable you actually are.

95
00:05:43,853 --> 00:05:47,300
You have simple working clothes and
practical gear.

96
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:51,133
A successful captain might display
expensive finery.

97
00:05:51,133 --> 00:05:54,946
The new sailor hauling a sack does not
suddenly own that captain's

98
00:05:54,946 --> 00:05:57,932
wardrobe, reputation, or much
better-looking boots.

99
00:05:58,670 --> 00:06:00,721
Finally, you leave the harbor.

100
00:06:00,721 --> 00:06:05,057
Houses shrink behind you, the shore slips
away, and for a

101
00:06:05,057 --> 00:06:07,829
moment the horizon looks wonderfully open.

102
00:06:07,829 --> 00:06:11,095
The first instruction is not to admire the
horizon.

103
00:06:11,095 --> 00:06:15,018
You handle a line while another sailor
works beside you.

104
00:06:15,018 --> 00:06:19,106
The vessel needs coordination. It is not
impressed by your

105
00:06:19,106 --> 00:06:21,364
private discovery that the ocean is large.

106
00:06:21,364 --> 00:06:24,517
When the wind shifts, the work changes
with it.

107
00:06:24,517 --> 00:06:28,601
Sail handling, steering, lookout duties,
and repairs remain

108
00:06:28,601 --> 00:06:32,678
necessary. A pirate ship is still a ship,
which is a detail

109
00:06:32,678 --> 00:06:36,740
the recruitment speech underemphasized.
You climb carefully to work

110
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:40,684
where you are needed. The deck moves
below, rope presses into your

111
00:06:40,684 --> 00:06:44,509
hands, and the height becomes much more
interesting now that there is

112
00:06:44,509 --> 00:06:48,198
no modern safety briefing. Back down, you
scrub the deck.

113
00:06:49,082 --> 00:06:53,435
Cleanliness cannot eliminate every danger,
but neglecting the living

114
00:06:53,435 --> 00:06:54,980
space will not improve it.

115
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:58,534
Then it is your turn at less pleasant work
below.

116
00:06:58,534 --> 00:07:02,925
Water collects, stores need attention, and
the ship has

117
00:07:02,925 --> 00:07:06,155
smells nobody wants to describe
poetically.

118
00:07:06,894 --> 00:07:09,753
The bucket has returned to the center of
your professional life.

119
00:07:10,283 --> 00:07:13,492
Breakfast brings ship's biscuit and
preserved food.

120
00:07:13,492 --> 00:07:17,818
On longer voyages, sailors carried
provisions that could keep,

121
00:07:17,818 --> 00:07:22,173
including salted meat. This solved part of
the storage problem while

122
00:07:22,173 --> 00:07:24,683
creating a fairly repetitive dining
experience.

123
00:07:24,683 --> 00:07:27,055
You inspect the biscuit before eating it.

124
00:07:27,617 --> 00:07:32,072
Dry provisions are useful, but stored food
can deteriorate or acquire

125
00:07:32,072 --> 00:07:35,936
unwanted residents. Your meal has an
unsettling possibility of

126
00:07:35,936 --> 00:07:38,675
containing something that considers the
biscuit its house.

127
00:07:39,317 --> 00:07:42,184
The salted meat is not a fresh dinner
straight from a cheerful

128
00:07:42,184 --> 00:07:46,426
kitchen. You eat what is available,
remembering that the crew cannot

129
00:07:46,426 --> 00:07:49,572
order replacements from the nearest
invisible supermarket in the

130
00:07:49,572 --> 00:07:53,204
middle of the sea. Water must last and
remain usable.

131
00:07:53,204 --> 00:07:56,430
Heat and storage can make provisions less
pleasant.

132
00:07:56,430 --> 00:08:00,593
The quartermaster checking supplies is
therefore doing something more

133
00:08:00,593 --> 00:08:04,396
important than standing beside barrels
with an official expression.

134
00:08:04,396 --> 00:08:08,724
You start dreaming about fresh fruit, then
about a quiet

135
00:08:08,724 --> 00:08:12,952
bed. After a while, the fantasy of gold
has acquired several

136
00:08:12,952 --> 00:08:17,354
extremely ordinary conditions. Over
prolonged periods without enough

137
00:08:17,354 --> 00:08:21,323
fresh, suitable food, another danger could
appear:

138
00:08:21,323 --> 00:08:25,815
scurvy. Royal Museums Greenwich explains
that

139
00:08:25,815 --> 00:08:29,084
it results from a sustained lack of
vitamin C.

140
00:08:29,084 --> 00:08:31,029
It was a serious threat to sailors.

141
00:08:31,783 --> 00:08:35,218
Weakness and painful symptoms could make
work harder before the

142
00:08:35,298 --> 00:08:39,680
illness became fatal. This was not
something every pirate developed

143
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:43,705
after missing one orange. Diet and time
mattered, and supplies

144
00:08:43,705 --> 00:08:48,193
differed between voyages. Your crew does
not understand vitamins in

145
00:08:48,193 --> 00:08:52,130
the modern sense. Fresh food can help, but
a reliable scientific

146
00:08:52,130 --> 00:08:55,080
solution is not sitting in your medical
cupboard.

147
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:59,575
A beautifully brave attitude does not
replace missing

148
00:08:59,575 --> 00:09:03,831
nutrition. Nor does rum. Pirates were not
permanently drunk,

149
00:09:03,831 --> 00:09:08,159
whatever the costume suggests. Drink might
offer pleasure, but

150
00:09:08,159 --> 00:09:11,788
handling equipment on a moving vessel
while helplessly

151
00:09:11,788 --> 00:09:16,085
intoxicated introduces a very practical
disagreement with

152
00:09:16,085 --> 00:09:19,786
gravity. At night, you settle into your
sleeping space.

153
00:09:19,786 --> 00:09:24,094
Someone nearby snores; another man shifts;
the ship creaks.

154
00:09:25,057 --> 00:09:28,906
You have paid for your escape from
ordinary life with exceptionally

155
00:09:28,906 --> 00:09:31,583
close access to other people's noises.

156
00:09:31,583 --> 00:09:33,874
You are woken for your next duty.

157
00:09:33,874 --> 00:09:38,071
Rest depends on the needs of the ship, not
simply on when

158
00:09:38,071 --> 00:09:42,400
you feel finished. The sea has never
agreed to respect your

159
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:46,355
preferred working hours. A darker sky
brings more work.

160
00:09:46,355 --> 00:09:50,530
The crew adjusts sail and secures what
must stay in

161
00:09:50,530 --> 00:09:55,004
place. You watch rain sweep across the
deck and understand that

162
00:09:55,004 --> 00:09:58,469
threatening strangers is irrelevant to
controlling weather.

163
00:09:58,469 --> 00:10:02,870
Water comes aboard, a loose object slides,
and you help keep

164
00:10:02,870 --> 00:10:07,324
things under control. This is not
automatically a spectacular

165
00:10:07,324 --> 00:10:11,262
shipwreck. Sometimes danger means an
exhausting afternoon

166
00:10:11,262 --> 00:10:14,405
preventing the much worse version from
happening.

167
00:10:14,405 --> 00:10:16,616
When the weather eases, you inspect
damage.

168
00:10:17,209 --> 00:10:20,236
A torn piece of cloth and strained gear
are enough to demand

169
00:10:20,236 --> 00:10:24,318
attention. Surviving trouble rarely means
you are now entitled to a

170
00:10:24,318 --> 00:10:28,476
completely restful afternoon. Days pass
without a valuable prize

171
00:10:28,524 --> 00:10:32,512
appearing. You still eat, drink, work, and
use

172
00:10:32,512 --> 00:10:36,702
supplies. The sea does not owe you a
profitable encounter.

173
00:10:36,702 --> 00:10:41,055
Eventually, another sail appears. The
captain and crew

174
00:10:41,055 --> 00:10:43,011
must judge an uncertain situation.

175
00:10:43,011 --> 00:10:47,275
You are looking at another working vessel,
with people aboard who have

176
00:10:47,275 --> 00:10:49,730
their own reasons for wanting to arrive
safely.

177
00:10:50,388 --> 00:10:53,179
The popular image focuses on spectacular
battles.

178
00:10:53,693 --> 00:10:57,143
Intimidation could sometimes produce
surrender without one.

179
00:10:57,143 --> 00:11:00,922
That did not make piracy harmless: the
threat itself could take

180
00:11:00,922 --> 00:11:04,569
property and control away from people
facing it.

181
00:11:04,569 --> 00:11:08,615
In our imagined encounter, the other crew
surrenders.

182
00:11:08,615 --> 00:11:12,770
We do not need a dramatic sword duel to
understand the stakes.

183
00:11:12,770 --> 00:11:16,649
The people standing nearby are frightened,
and they have excellent

184
00:11:16,649 --> 00:11:21,043
reasons to be. You see a merchant sailor
watching his belongings

185
00:11:21,043 --> 00:11:25,092
being taken. Until recently, your working
life looked

186
00:11:25,092 --> 00:11:28,284
closer to his than to the captain's
fantasy.

187
00:11:28,284 --> 00:11:32,294
An improved career prospect feels less
charming from this

188
00:11:32,294 --> 00:11:34,448
distance. The cargo is inspected.

189
00:11:35,122 --> 00:11:39,022
Trade goods were often more important than
heaps of gold: cloth,

190
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:43,645
drink, supplies, and other things that had
buyers ashore.

191
00:11:44,495 --> 00:11:47,689
Wealth can be valuable while remaining
awkward to carry.

192
00:11:48,363 --> 00:11:50,147
You help move a bulky bundle.

193
00:11:50,147 --> 00:11:54,499
Nothing about its shape resembles treasure
from an adventure film.

194
00:11:54,499 --> 00:11:58,614
It resembles extra lifting. Even when
piracy produces a

195
00:11:58,614 --> 00:12:01,807
result, somebody still has to do the
loading.

196
00:12:01,807 --> 00:12:05,936
A damaged container proves less useful
than everyone hoped.

197
00:12:05,936 --> 00:12:10,336
Captured goods are not necessarily intact,
convenient, or easy to

198
00:12:10,336 --> 00:12:14,542
sell. The crew has not discovered an
unlimited supply of portable

199
00:12:14,542 --> 00:12:18,295
money. The quartermaster compares supplies
and looks after the

200
00:12:18,295 --> 00:12:21,992
division that will follow. Your imagined
crew's arrangements matter

201
00:12:21,992 --> 00:12:25,864
now. The share you expected must come from
what is actually there,

202
00:12:26,457 --> 00:12:28,682
not what sounded attractive ashore.

203
00:12:28,682 --> 00:12:31,699
Captured people face uncertainty as well.

204
00:12:31,699 --> 00:12:34,937
Some sailors were invited or pressured to
join pirate crews.

205
00:12:35,610 --> 00:12:38,983
Their choices could happen under fear,
which is very different from

206
00:12:38,983 --> 00:12:42,396
freely comparing interesting employment
opportunities.

207
00:12:42,396 --> 00:12:46,340
And enslaved people were not automatically
rescued by a pirate flag.

208
00:12:46,998 --> 00:12:50,979
The museum warns that pirates often
continued their exploitation

209
00:12:51,332 --> 00:12:53,074
or sold them for profit.

210
00:12:53,074 --> 00:12:57,119
The myth of universal liberation hides
deeply ugly

211
00:12:57,119 --> 00:13:01,183
realities. Our story does not make Tom a
heroic

212
00:13:01,183 --> 00:13:03,400
savior to excuse that history.

213
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:06,294
Your discomfort cannot undo the harm.

214
00:13:07,064 --> 00:13:10,672
A crew's rules for sharing spoils say
little about the rights of

215
00:13:10,672 --> 00:13:15,023
people outside that crew. Once the
encounter ends, you

216
00:13:15,023 --> 00:13:17,220
still have a ship to manage and stores to
ration.

217
00:13:18,134 --> 00:13:22,313
One prize has not cancelled weather,
hunger, sickness, or the

218
00:13:22,313 --> 00:13:25,726
possibility of meeting somebody far better
armed.

219
00:13:25,726 --> 00:13:28,582
The captain decides to return toward
shore.

220
00:13:28,582 --> 00:13:32,773
You have something that may be valuable,
but value needs buyers

221
00:13:32,966 --> 00:13:37,247
and practical arrangements. A bundle of
expensive cloth cannot

222
00:13:37,247 --> 00:13:40,069
itself repair the vessel or feed
everybody.

223
00:13:40,824 --> 00:13:44,121
Approaching a harbor is not the same as
arriving at a welcoming

224
00:13:44,121 --> 00:13:48,528
resort. Local authorities, merchants, and
powerful interests

225
00:13:48,528 --> 00:13:52,930
vary. A place willing to deal with pirates
today may become a

226
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:55,308
much less comfortable place later.

227
00:13:55,308 --> 00:13:56,864
You go ashore with the crew.

228
00:13:57,522 --> 00:14:00,670
The ground feels strange after so much
movement.

229
00:14:00,670 --> 00:14:04,829
For a moment, your ambitions become
wonderfully small:

230
00:14:04,829 --> 00:14:08,969
fresh food, dry clothes, and a place to
sit that stays still.

231
00:14:10,108 --> 00:14:13,382
At a waterfront stall, a dealer examines
the goods.

232
00:14:14,136 --> 00:14:18,619
You watch the negotiation while mentally
spending money that does not

233
00:14:18,619 --> 00:14:22,225
yet belong to you. There are practical
costs before personal

234
00:14:22,725 --> 00:14:27,216
luxury. The vessel needs supplies and
repairs, and the agreed

235
00:14:27,216 --> 00:14:31,436
division must be respected. Your fictional
reckoning leaves you

236
00:14:31,436 --> 00:14:35,674
with a modest share, not instant ownership
of a mansion.

237
00:14:35,674 --> 00:14:39,582
No single payout table describes every
pirate crew.

238
00:14:39,582 --> 00:14:43,565
Different articles and circumstances
produced different

239
00:14:43,565 --> 00:14:47,996
arrangements. A later famous captain's
recorded agreement cannot

240
00:14:47,996 --> 00:14:52,394
automatically tell us exactly what this
imagined sailor earns

241
00:14:52,394 --> 00:14:56,682
in seventeen fifteen. You count what you
have, then look at what

242
00:14:56,682 --> 00:15:00,940
ordinary necessities will cost. Now your
complaint concerns the

243
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:03,784
difficulty of keeping newly earned money.

244
00:15:03,784 --> 00:15:07,009
You buy a plain replacement shirt and
fresh food.

245
00:15:07,009 --> 00:15:11,486
The shirt fits reasonably well; the food
tastes astonishingly

246
00:15:11,486 --> 00:15:15,644
good. If adventure had advertised these
rewards accurately, its poster

247
00:15:15,644 --> 00:15:18,212
would have looked rather less dramatic.

248
00:15:18,212 --> 00:15:22,088
Back in the tavern, someone wants to
celebrate.

249
00:15:22,088 --> 00:15:26,405
Royal Museums Greenwich notes that

250
00:15:26,405 --> 00:15:30,281
pirates commonly spent their proceeds
rather than burying

251
00:15:30,281 --> 00:15:34,613
elaborate treasure hoards. Your purse does
not become safer just

252
00:15:34,613 --> 00:15:36,483
because the evening becomes louder.

253
00:15:37,270 --> 00:15:41,043
You pay for a meal and stop before your
small fortune becomes

254
00:15:41,543 --> 00:15:44,557
a story about how generous you were last
night.

255
00:15:44,557 --> 00:15:47,073
Other men make different decisions.

256
00:15:47,073 --> 00:15:50,751
Tomorrow's regret is already waiting
outside the door.

257
00:15:50,751 --> 00:15:53,575
The next morning, there is work on the
ship.

258
00:15:53,575 --> 00:15:57,970
Repairs do not become unnecessary because
everyone enjoyed being

259
00:15:57,970 --> 00:16:02,077
ashore. You hold a piece of worn sail
while another crew member

260
00:16:02,077 --> 00:16:04,535
helps patch it. The hull also needs
attention.

261
00:16:05,129 --> 00:16:09,291
A wooden vessel lives in water and carries
the consequences of every

262
00:16:09,291 --> 00:16:13,495
hard passage. The captain cannot order the
material to become

263
00:16:13,495 --> 00:16:17,828
permanently indestructible. An argument
starts over the previous

264
00:16:17,828 --> 00:16:22,277
division. The quartermaster listens while
two men make increasingly

265
00:16:22,277 --> 00:16:26,162
confident claims. Articles are useful
partly because human memory

266
00:16:26,162 --> 00:16:29,964
becomes remarkably creative whenever money
enters the room.

267
00:16:29,964 --> 00:16:33,518
You get a chance to speak, but agreement
is not automatic.

268
00:16:33,518 --> 00:16:37,789
Some crews limited captains' power and
settled disputes

269
00:16:37,789 --> 00:16:42,157
collectively. That is not proof that this
ship, or any other,

270
00:16:42,157 --> 00:16:46,169
lacked threats and punishment. Nor was
discipline

271
00:16:46,169 --> 00:16:48,797
confined to a theatrical walk along a
plank.

272
00:16:49,615 --> 00:16:53,337
The familiar plank scene belongs chiefly
to later storytelling.

273
00:16:54,252 --> 00:16:58,492
Real punishments could be harsh;
abandoning a person ashore was

274
00:16:58,492 --> 00:17:02,977
one documented possibility. You decide
that being left alone on an

275
00:17:02,977 --> 00:17:06,948
uninhabited island would make your earlier
accommodation complaints

276
00:17:06,948 --> 00:17:10,832
seem optimistic. The rules you disliked
deserve

277
00:17:10,832 --> 00:17:14,886
attention. This is not a club you can
treat casually.

278
00:17:14,886 --> 00:17:19,363
Meanwhile, news reaches the waterfront
about authorities pursuing

279
00:17:19,363 --> 00:17:22,538
pirates. Governments had strong reasons to
protect trade.

280
00:17:22,538 --> 00:17:26,877
You may feel small and unimportant, but
participating in piracy

281
00:17:26,877 --> 00:17:30,384
gives those governments an interest in
your future.

282
00:17:30,384 --> 00:17:34,621
Capture can mean trial and severe
punishment, including hanging.

283
00:17:34,621 --> 00:17:38,849
You do not need to witness an execution to
understand the

284
00:17:38,849 --> 00:17:43,160
consequence. The possibility follows you
into ordinary moments,

285
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:47,143
even while buying breakfast. In the next
few years,

286
00:17:47,143 --> 00:17:51,566
campaigns and pardons would intensify the
pressure on Atlantic

287
00:17:51,566 --> 00:17:55,757
piracy. Some pirates accepted offers to
stop; others

288
00:17:55,757 --> 00:18:00,054
continued. We should not insert a later
pardon into your current

289
00:18:00,054 --> 00:18:04,235
day as guaranteed protection. A formidable
armed vessel

290
00:18:04,235 --> 00:18:08,568
appears offshore, and your crew postpones
its next departure.

291
00:18:08,568 --> 00:18:11,902
In this fictional moment, caution wins.

292
00:18:11,902 --> 00:18:16,020
You are relieved. The decision also means
more

293
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:20,217
waiting, uncertainty, and supplies
consumed without a return.

294
00:18:20,217 --> 00:18:21,885
You look at the ship from the quay.

295
00:18:22,576 --> 00:18:25,643
It promised freedom, and parts of that
promise appealed to you.

296
00:18:26,429 --> 00:18:30,501
Yet freedom from one employer has not
delivered freedom from other

297
00:18:30,501 --> 00:18:33,637
people, material needs, or consequences.

298
00:18:34,551 --> 00:18:37,143
Another sailor mentions legitimate work.

299
00:18:37,143 --> 00:18:40,872
It might pay poorly and involve a captain
you dislike.

300
00:18:40,872 --> 00:18:45,268
It would also avoid some dangers created
specifically by belonging

301
00:18:45,381 --> 00:18:49,607
to this crew. Neither option arrives
wearing a perfect future.

302
00:18:49,607 --> 00:18:52,616
Your individual fate remains imagined.

303
00:18:53,226 --> 00:18:56,595
What matters is the bargain you finally
understand:

304
00:18:57,398 --> 00:19:01,507
uncertain rewards, constant labor, serious

305
00:19:01,507 --> 00:19:05,932
risks, and opportunities built partly on
the suffering

306
00:19:05,932 --> 00:19:09,374
of people you were encouraged not to think
about.

307
00:19:09,374 --> 00:19:13,562
The famous names survive because they make
remarkable stories.

308
00:19:13,562 --> 00:19:18,008
The ordinary sailor with sore hands, damp
clothes,

309
00:19:18,008 --> 00:19:20,778
and a modest share is easier to overlook.

310
00:19:21,565 --> 00:19:24,872
He still did most of the uncomfortable
living.

311
00:19:24,872 --> 00:19:28,231
So why was being a pirate worse than you
think?

312
00:19:28,231 --> 00:19:32,625
Because treasure was uncertain, freedom
had limits, and

313
00:19:32,625 --> 00:19:36,641
even your dangerous new life needed
somebody to clean

314
00:19:36,770 --> 00:19:39,872
below deck. That bucket is waiting.

315
00:19:40,772 --> 00:19:44,614
This is Pirate Pages: the lives behind the
legends.
